# Steam Credentials Template
# 
# INSTRUCTIONS:
# 1. Copy this file to steam_credentials.txt
# 2. Replace the placeholder text below with your actual Steam credentials
# 3. Remove or keep the comment lines (lines starting with #) - they will be skipped
# 4. Ensure your username is on the first non-comment line
# 5. Ensure your password is on the second non-comment line
#
# IMPORTANT: steam_credentials.txt is in .gitignore and will NOT be committed to the repository

# Line 1: Your Steam username (replace the text below)
your_steam_username_here

# Line 2: Your Steam password (optional - see security notes below)
# If you leave this as placeholder text or blank, SteamCMD will prompt you for it interactively
your_steam_password_here

# SECURITY NOTES:
# 
# 1. RECOMMENDED: Use Steam Guard for secure authentication
#    - Enable Steam Guard on your account (Settings > Account > Manage Steam Guard Account Security)
#    - On first login, SteamCMD will prompt for your Steam Guard code
#    - After successful authentication, SteamCMD stores the session locally
#    - Future logins won't require password or Steam Guard code
#
# 2. Password storage (less secure):
#    - If you store password here, ensure file permissions are restricted
#    - Never commit this file to version control
#    - Consider using environment variables instead
#
# 3. ALTERNATIVE: Use "+login <username>" without password
#    - SteamCMD will prompt for password interactively
#    - More secure than storing password in plain text
#
# 4. For CI/CD pipelines:
#    - Use Steam Guard with stored credentials
#    - Or use environment variables with secret management
#    - Never store credentials in build scripts
